\section{The literal scheduler and its subtraction boundary}

Let $n,k\geq1$ and $\Words_{n,k}=[k]^n$.  We first fix the signature
convention, since reversing a word or reversing the tensor convention changes
which occurrence an operator edits.  For $1\leq i<k$ and
$w=w_1\cdots w_n$, ignore letters outside $\{i,i+1\}$, write a plus sign for
each $i$ and a minus sign for each $i+1$, and repeatedly delete matched pairs
$+-$, with ignored letters allowed between the signs.  The remaining signs
are a string of minuses followed by a string of pluses.  If a minus remains,
$e_i(w)$ changes the position of the \emph{rightmost} unpaired $i+1$ into
$i$; otherwise $e_i(w)$ is undefined.  If a plus remains, $f_i(w)$ changes
the position of the \emph{leftmost} unpaired $i$ into $i+1$; otherwise
$f_i(w)$ is undefined.  These partial maps are mutual inverses along every
$i$-string.

Define $F=F_{n,k}:\Words_{n,k}\to\Words_{n,k}$ by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:update}
 F(w)=
 \begin{cases}
 e_{m(w)}(w),&m(w)=\min\{i:e_i(w)\text{ is defined}\}\text{ exists},\\
 w,&\text{all }e_i(w)\text{ are undefined}.
 \end{cases}
\end{equation}
Thus the state is re-examined after every individual crystal edge.  For
example, when $k=3$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:example}
333\xrightarrow{2}332\xrightarrow{1}331\xrightarrow{2}321
\xrightarrow{1}311\xrightarrow{2}211\xrightarrow{1}111,
\end{equation}
where the superscript is the selected colour.

